68
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,880
So, what does probability language do?

69
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:00,880
It allows us to quantify uncertainty.

70
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:02,880
So we live in an uncertain world.

71
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:05,880
Very often, when someone asks you a question,

72
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:07,880
it's tempting just to say, I don't know.

73
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:09,880
But there is more that you could say.

74
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:11,880
There is more nuance that you can add

75
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:13,880
to your I don't know statements.

76
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,880
And what probability language allows us to do

77
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,880
is to actually put a number on it.

78
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:21,880
To go and say, well, look, yes, I don't know.

79
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,880
But what's your probability of having it?

80
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:27,880
90%, or 10%, or 3%, or 1 in 100 billion.

81
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:31,880
These are all possible probabilities.

82
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:34,880
Now, there are two different interpretations of probability.

83
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:37,880
There is the least controversial one,

84
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:39,880
which is objective probability.

85
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:41,880
Objective probability.

86
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,880
All right, so even when we don't know what's going to happen,

87
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:47,880
we can definitely talk about the relative frequencies

88
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:50,880
of various observed events in the past.

89
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:54,880
And if you know any Austrian economists here,

90
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:56,880
or if you read them, they will say,

91
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:58,880
we have no problem with objective probability.

92
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,880
Objective probability is fine.

93
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,880
So all you're doing is getting a tally

94
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:05,880
of everything that's ever happened,

95
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:08,880
and then going and saying, this is the percentage of times

96
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,880
that all the doors I've ever seen are open,

97
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,880
the percentage of times that they were closed.

98
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:15,880
So the objective probability that a door is closed

99
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:18,880
is the ratio of times that I saw doors closed

100
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:20,880
to the number of doors that I've ever seen.

101
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,880
OK, something like that.

102
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,880
All right, so you can always talk about relative frequencies.

103
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:29,880
But objective probability, though there are no

104
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,880
philosophical complaints about it,

105
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,880
is problematic in a lot of ways.

106
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,880
The biggest problem is that according to objective probability,

107
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:40,880
you cannot talk about the frequency of unique events.

108
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,880
Because unique events has either already happened,

109
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:45,880
in which case it's got a probability of 0 or 1.

110
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:48,880
But then this doesn't tell us anything about anything

111
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:50,880
that's ever going to happen, because those events

112
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,880
are going to be unique events too.

113
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:54,880
All right, so that is the problem.

114
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:56,880
And here's the thing.

115
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,880
If you really take the idea seriously,

116
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:02,880
how many real world events are in fact unique?

117
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:05,880
All events are unique.

118
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:08,880
This event is unique, just like every other event.

119
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:11,880
If you just think about, well,

120
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:14,880
so what is the, like, all computer monitors

121
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:17,880
in this room that have ever been in this room

122
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:19,880
that have been tipped over by me

123
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:22,880
on the first Monday of class in 2017,

124
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:24,880
and now we're already down to unique events.

125
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:27,880
It's the only one that fits that description.

126
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,880
Or if you go and apply it even to games of chance,

127
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:33,880
you say, what's the probability of royal flush?

128
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,880
Well, what's the probability of getting a royal flush

129
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:39,880
tonight in the MGM Grand Casino

130
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,880
when a guy in a top hat just walked by,

131
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,880
when Baz Luhrmann's musical is playing

132
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:47,880
across the street in three hours,

133
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,880
when just add on a number of conditions

134
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:52,880
and pretty soon you've described a unique event.

135
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,880
And then you have the objective probability

136
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,880
of something that is never going,

137
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:58,880
that you see that it will never happen again.

138
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,880
So then what is the use?

139
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:02,880
In any event you can describe that way.

140
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:04,880
For anything that's happened, you can say,

141
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:06,880
well, once you add some detail on.

142
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:08,880
I remember when I was a kid, sometimes I would get

143
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:10,880
this sense of metaphysical awe when I'd realize,

144
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,880
you know, I'm probably the only person in the world today

145
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:17,880
who read Encyclopedia Brown

146
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:20,880
and then went and watched the Power Rangers

147
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:24,880
and then went and got punched by his brother Gregory Randolph

148
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:27,880
and then went and fed his cat, B-I-C,

149
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:29,880
which stands for very important cat.

150
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:31,880
So this is probably the only person,

151
00:06:31,880 --> 00:06:33,880
I'm the only person out of billions,

152
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:35,880
and I'm the only one, I'm unique.

153
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:37,880
And that was like, wow.

154
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:40,880
You just need to add on a few layers of detail

155
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:46,880
or if you have ever listened to your music mix on Shuffle,

156
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:48,880
five songs, what are the odds that those five songs

157
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:50,880
have ever been listened to by anybody else on earth

158
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:53,880
in that exact order?

159
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:55,880
Probably very low, unless you're like,

160
00:06:55,880 --> 00:07:00,880
you've only won an album on your player.

161
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,880
Okay.

162
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:05,880
Alright, so anyway, if you take this idea seriously,

163
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,880
you realize every event is strictly speaking unique,

164
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:09,880
so you can never apply probability to the real world.

165
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:11,880
When I made this point in a debate with Walter Block,

166
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:14,880
he just said, great, even better.

167
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:16,880
I never liked probability anyway.

168
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:19,880
So you can't even apply it to poker anymore.

169
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:20,880
You can't apply it to the lottery.

170
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:22,880
You can't apply it to dice.

171
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:24,880
So it just doesn't apply.

172
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:27,880
Of course, most people find this idea odd.

173
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:29,880
You can never talk about probabilities of anything.

174
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:35,880
You can't say, look, well, we've never played the lottery

175
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:39,880
on February 1st, 2017, so we can't actually talk

176
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,880
about the probability of winning.

177
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:42,880
It's just not allowed.

178
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:43,880
There's no way we can say it.

179
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:45,880
Any claim about it is meaningless.

180
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:49,880
So that seems like a strange conclusion,

181
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,880
although you can always bite these bullets if you want to.

182
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:55,880
Alright, so this leads us to a much more useful

183
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:58,880
but of course more controversial concept of probability,

184
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,880
which is subject to probability.

185
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:03,880
Subject to probability is simply the degree of belief

186
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,880
a person assigns to a proposition, and that is all.

187
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,880
Alright, probability is some simple axioms.

188
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:13,880
Beliefs range from impossible to certain,

189
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:15,880
possible corresponding to probability of zero,

190
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:16,880
certain to probability of one.

191
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:19,880
Are there any math majors in this room?

192
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:21,880
Okay, so if you are a math major,

193
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:22,880
you know that technically speaking,

194
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:24,880
an event can have a probability of one

195
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:26,880
but not be truly certain, and you can have a probability

196
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:28,880
of zero but not be impossible.

197
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:30,880
There could be an infinitesimal probability,

198
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:32,880
but we're not going to worry about that.

199
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:36,880
Alright, okay, and since something always happens,

200
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:38,880
right, something always happens,

201
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:42,880
then the sum of all possible events must equal one,

202
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:44,880
because it is certain that something will happen.

203
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:48,880
Okay, so the idea of subjective probability is for any event.

204
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:51,880
So how confident are you on the spectrum

205
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:54,880
from it being certain with probability of zero

206
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,880
to be certain or possible with probability of zero

207
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,880
to certain with probability of one?

208
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:01,880
You could be 0.9, so I assign probability of 0.9 to it,

209
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:03,880
probability of 0.3, 0.78,

210
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:05,880
there could be any probabilities that you can assign

211
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:07,880
to any event, right, all, and I'll have,

212
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,880
where do these probabilities come from?

213
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:11,880
Just from inside your head.

214
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:14,880
How confident are you of this?

215
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,880
Now of course, the fact that someone states

216
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:19,880
high or low confidence doesn't mean they really believe it.

217
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:22,880
One of the best ways of eliciting probabilities

218
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:26,880
that people genuinely, sincerely, honestly, earnestly believe

219
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:29,880
is to offer to bet them based upon their announced probabilities.

220
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:31,880
And if you know anything about me at all,

221
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:32,880
this is one of my favorite things to do,

222
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:34,880
is when people say things that I don't believe

223
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,880
even they believe, I challenge them to bet me on it.

224
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:40,880
And usually I find that when you do this,

225
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,880
people run for the hills like frightened rabbits

226
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:44,880
because they are just shooting their mouth off

227
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:48,880
and they are using language that appears

228
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:50,880
to be very quantitatively precise,

229
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:54,880
but actually is just hot air.

230
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:57,880
There's also a lot of great empirical research

231
00:09:57,880 --> 00:09:59,880
on subjective probabilities.

232
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:01,880
So beginning in, I believe, the 60s,

233
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,880
some psychologists went and tried to find out

234
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,880
for the average person, what does,

235
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:11,880
what does the English phrase probably mean in numbers?

236
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:14,880
So when someone says X will probably happen,

237
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:17,880
what probability between zero and one does that correspond to?

238
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,880
And what they found was, frighteningly,

239
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:25,880
the range is somewhere between 20 and 90% for people.

240
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:28,880
That's what they think of as probably.

241
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:31,880
And then there's many other things.

242
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:33,880
How about almost certain?

243
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:36,880
Almost certain varies from, like,

244
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,880
65% up to 100%.

245
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:42,880
And again, this is not just like the most and least extreme.

246
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:44,880
This is, again, this is maybe like

247
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:47,880
the 25th and 75th percentile of answers.

248
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:50,880
So, you know, like, to me,

249
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,880
at least I always thought, look,

250
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:55,880
probably clearly means 0.5, you know, greater than 0.5.

251
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:57,880
Like, how could it mean anything else?

252
00:10:57,880 --> 00:10:58,880
I don't know.

253
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,880
Actually, English is way vaguer than I realized, most people.

254
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:05,880
Now, this was actually done in the context of

255
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:07,880
the United States Intelligence Services,

256
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:09,880
and after it was pointed out, a suggestion was made

257
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:12,880
that they replace all this verbiage with numbers,

258
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:14,880
and a decision was made to not do that.

259
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:19,880
To keep using vague adjectives rather than precise numbers,

260
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:23,880
which does reduce conflict among the people making,

261
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:25,880
writing the reports,

